1
00:02:25,062 --> 00:02:27,231
Are we the first?

2
00:02:29,859 --> 00:02:32,862
Are we the first to arrive here?

3
00:02:50,004 --> 00:02:52,465
Mr. Wilson!

4
00:03:13,402 --> 00:03:15,488
Is the interpreter with you?

5
00:03:16,447 --> 00:03:21,827
I'm not a professional interpreter.
My occupation is cartography.

6
00:03:21,994 --> 00:03:23,579
I'm a mapmaker.

7
00:03:23,788 --> 00:03:26,707
Can you translate French into English
and English into French?

8
00:03:26,916 --> 00:03:30,086
Yes, I explained to the team leader
in Villa Hermosa.

9
00:03:30,294 --> 00:03:32,713
I'm just a little taken by surprise.

10
00:03:37,885 --> 00:03:41,222
How long have you been working
on this project?

11
00:03:41,889 --> 00:03:45,893
I've been with the American team
from the beginning.

12
00:03:46,227 --> 00:03:49,688
In fact, I saw you at the Montsoreau
conference which ended well...

13
00:03:49,855 --> 00:03:50,898
...especially for you.

14
00:03:51,899 --> 00:03:55,903
Especially for the French.
If it isn't too late, my congratulations.

15
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:01,283
They're all there! All of them!

16
00:04:12,628 --> 00:04:15,089
Check for landing marks!

17
00:04:39,613 --> 00:04:43,451
Mr. Lacombe wants the numbers
off the engine blocks!

18
00:04:46,662 --> 00:04:49,665
M-447148!

19
00:04:49,874 --> 00:04:54,211
W-445529!

20
00:04:54,378 --> 00:04:57,923
Number 445529, check!

21
00:04:58,132 --> 00:05:02,678
Approximately 46 gallons
in the right tank!

22
00:05:02,887 --> 00:05:04,972
447149, check!

23
00:05:05,139 --> 00:05:07,224
Tell me something.
What's happening here?

24
00:05:07,391 --> 00:05:09,810
- It's flight number 19!
- 19 what?

25
00:05:09,977 --> 00:05:14,023
It's that training mission from the
Naval air station at Fort Lauderdale.

26
00:05:14,190 --> 00:05:17,151
They were doing target runs
on an old hulk.

27
00:05:17,401 --> 00:05:19,737
Who flies crates like these
anymore?

28
00:05:19,904 --> 00:05:25,367
No one! These planes were reported
missing in 1945.

29
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:31,540
But it looks brand-new!

30
00:05:35,878 --> 00:05:37,755
Where's the pilot?

31
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:40,841
I don't understand!

32
00:05:41,508 --> 00:05:43,594
Where's the crew? Hey!

33
00:05:47,598 --> 00:05:50,809
How the hell did it get here?

34
00:06:23,492 --> 00:06:24,660
He says this guy's local.

35
00:06:24,826 --> 00:06:26,912
He was here last night.
He saw it happen.

36
00:06:50,435 --> 00:06:52,229
Sunburn?

37
00:07:08,161 --> 00:07:11,373
He says the sun came out
last night.

38
00:07:12,332 --> 00:07:15,043
He says it sang to him.

39
00:07:51,037 --> 00:07:53,248
Harry, keep an eye
on that point-out I gave you.

40
00:07:53,415 --> 00:07:55,584
He's on 122.5.

41
00:07:55,751 --> 00:07:57,627
I'll be right back.

42
00:07:59,004 --> 00:08:02,924
Indianapolis Center, you have any
traffic for Air East 31?

43
00:08:04,468 --> 00:08:05,719
Air East 31, negative.

44
00:08:05,886 --> 00:08:09,473
The only traffic I have
is a TWA L-1011...

45
00:08:09,639 --> 00:08:13,602
...in your 6 o'clock position,
range 15 miles...

46
00:08:13,769 --> 00:08:17,606
...and an Allegheny DC-9 in
your 12 o'clock position, 50 miles.

47
00:08:17,773 --> 00:08:20,942
Stand by. I'll take a look at
broadband, over.

48
00:08:21,568 --> 00:08:24,613
Air East 31 has traffic 2 o'clock,
slightly above and descending.

49
00:08:25,322 --> 00:08:26,740
Air East 31, roger.

50
00:08:26,907 --> 00:08:29,993
I have a primary target
about that position now.

51
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:32,245
I have no known high-altitude traffic.

52
00:08:32,412 --> 00:08:34,206
Stand by, I'll check low, over.

53
00:08:34,956 --> 00:08:37,083
Dick, check and see
if they know who this is.

54
00:08:37,250 --> 00:08:38,919
Center, Air East 31.

55
00:08:39,127 --> 00:08:41,129
The traffic's not lower than us.

56
00:08:41,379 --> 00:08:44,800
He's 1 o'clock now,
still above me and descending.

57
00:08:45,509 --> 00:08:48,512
Air East 31,
can you say aircraft type?

58
00:08:49,054 --> 00:08:53,225
Negative, Center,
no distinct outline.

59
00:08:54,142 --> 00:08:56,937
To tell you the truth,
the target is rather brilliant.

60
00:08:57,103 --> 00:09:01,107
It has the brightest anti-collision
lights I think I've ever seen.

61
00:09:01,274 --> 00:09:03,693
Alternating white to red.

62
00:09:03,860 --> 00:09:05,570
The colors are a little striking.

63
00:09:05,737 --> 00:09:08,990
Center, this is TWA 517.

64
00:09:09,157 --> 00:09:12,410
Traffic now looks like
extra-bright landing lights.

65
00:09:12,577 --> 00:09:16,081
I thought Air East
had his landing lights on.

66
00:09:17,123 --> 00:09:18,625
Could be a satellite.

67
00:09:18,792 --> 00:09:21,837
I have that primary target now
in your 10 o'clock position...

68
00:09:22,003 --> 00:09:23,046
...five miles, over.

69
00:09:23,213 --> 00:09:25,465
- Never seen anything like that.
- Affirmative, 31.

70
00:09:25,632 --> 00:09:29,010
The traffic is proceeding northeast.
No altitude readout.

71
00:09:29,177 --> 00:09:32,097
Roger, Center. It doesn't appear
he's gonna be a problem.

72
00:09:32,264 --> 00:09:35,767
He's just descended
about 1500 feet below me.

73
00:09:35,934 --> 00:09:37,686
Wait a second!

74
00:09:37,853 --> 00:09:39,062
Stand by one.

75
00:09:39,688 --> 00:09:42,524
Okay, Center, Air East 31.
The traffic has turned.

76
00:09:42,691 --> 00:09:46,236
He's heading right for my windshield.
We're turning right immediately.

77
00:09:48,029 --> 00:09:50,365
Air East 31, descend
and maintain flight level 310.

78
00:09:50,532 --> 00:09:52,325
Break Allegheny,
turn right 30 degrees.

79
00:09:52,492 --> 00:09:54,286
Get on the horn
to the 45th Recon Wing.

80
00:09:54,452 --> 00:09:57,205
See what the hell
they're testing up there.

81
00:09:57,372 --> 00:10:01,376
This is Indianapolis Center, do you have
test operations in restricted area 2508?

82
00:10:01,585 --> 00:10:03,128
Air East 31, roger.

83
00:10:03,295 --> 00:10:07,465
The traffic is quite luminous and is
exhibiting some non-ballistic motion.

84
00:10:07,632 --> 00:10:10,260
Roger. Continue descent
at your discretion, over.

85
00:10:10,427 --> 00:10:13,513
Okay, Center. Descent
at pilot's discretion is approved.

86
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:16,892
The traffic is approaching head-on,
ultra-bright and really moving.

87
00:10:17,058 --> 00:10:20,604
And right by us! Right now!
Now, that was really close.

88
00:10:20,770 --> 00:10:23,815
Air East 31 is out of 340...

89
00:10:24,024 --> 00:10:26,151
Ask them if they want
to report officially.

90
00:10:26,568 --> 00:10:30,447
TWA 517, do you want to report
a UFO? Over.

91
00:10:34,743 --> 00:10:38,872
TWA 517, do you want to report
a UFO? Over.

92
00:10:40,498 --> 00:10:43,627
Negative. We don't want to report.

93
00:10:43,793 --> 00:10:47,339
Air East 31,
do you wish to report a UFO? Over.

94
00:10:48,924 --> 00:10:51,593
Negative. We don't want
to report one of those either.

95
00:10:51,968 --> 00:10:56,056
Air East 31, do you wish to
file a report of any kind? Over.

96
00:10:56,514 --> 00:10:59,476
I wouldn't know what kind
of report to file, Center.

97
00:10:59,643 --> 00:11:01,728
Air East 31, me neither.

98
00:11:01,895 --> 00:11:04,397
I'll try to track traffic
to destination, over.

99
00:13:33,755 --> 00:13:36,758
- What are you doing?
- I'm just running my way...

100
00:13:40,470 --> 00:13:42,347
What?

101
00:13:51,481 --> 00:13:53,191
Barry?

102
00:14:03,910 --> 00:14:05,537
Honey?

103
00:14:15,630 --> 00:14:17,298
Honey?

104
00:14:20,885 --> 00:14:22,762
Barry!

105
00:14:24,722 --> 00:14:26,641
Barry.

106
00:14:27,517 --> 00:14:29,018
Barry!

107
00:14:42,708 --> 00:14:45,794
Dad, do my problems for me.

108
00:14:47,087 --> 00:14:51,174
I don't have to do your problems for
you. You do your problems for you.

109
00:14:51,341 --> 00:14:55,178
That's why I graduated,
so I don't have to do problems.

110
00:14:55,554 --> 00:14:58,098
I don't understand these fractions.

111
00:14:58,265 --> 00:15:01,977
All right. What's one-third of 60?

112
00:15:02,352 --> 00:15:04,354
That's a fraction.
I don't understand them.

113
00:15:04,521 --> 00:15:06,106
All right, look.

114
00:15:06,273 --> 00:15:08,817
Let's say...

115
00:15:08,984 --> 00:15:12,863
- ...that this boxcar is 60 feet long, okay?
- Okay.

116
00:15:13,363 --> 00:15:16,491
And one-third of it is across
this switch here.

117
00:15:16,658 --> 00:15:19,786
All right? Okay?

118
00:15:19,953 --> 00:15:23,957
And now another train is coming.

119
00:15:25,417 --> 00:15:29,087
Now, how far do you have to move
this boxcar off the track...

120
00:15:29,254 --> 00:15:31,798
...so that the other train
doesn't smash it?

121
00:15:31,965 --> 00:15:34,843
Quickly, Brad, there are thousands
of lives at stake.

122
00:15:35,510 --> 00:15:37,596
Brad, any answer.

123
00:15:37,888 --> 00:15:38,931
Honey?

124
00:15:39,681 --> 00:15:42,100
Remember last weekend...

125
00:15:42,267 --> 00:15:46,146
...you promised everybody a movie
this weekend?

126
00:15:47,856 --> 00:15:49,399
Honey?

127
00:15:51,860 --> 00:15:55,781
- And you also promised Goofy Golf.
- Oh, yeah.

128
00:16:00,535 --> 00:16:04,539
Roy, what is all this stuff
on my table?

129
00:16:04,706 --> 00:16:07,209
I thought I told you
this was for my stuff, this table.

130
00:16:07,376 --> 00:16:09,294
I mean, you can have that table.

131
00:16:09,461 --> 00:16:11,129
I don't want this stuff on my table.

132
00:16:11,296 --> 00:16:13,590
This can cause tetanus!

133
00:16:13,799 --> 00:16:16,343
- What is this?
- Hey, you know what's playing in town?

134
00:16:16,510 --> 00:16:19,346
Pinocchio!
The kids have never seen Pinocchio.

135
00:16:19,513 --> 00:16:22,307
You guys have never seen Pinocchio.
You're gonna love it.

136
00:16:22,474 --> 00:16:23,600
I don't believe this.

137
00:16:23,767 --> 00:16:28,563
Who wants to go see
some dumb cartoon rated G for kids?

138
00:16:28,730 --> 00:16:30,649
- How old are you?
- Eight.

139
00:16:30,816 --> 00:16:32,567
- You wanna be 9?
- Yeah.

140
00:16:32,734 --> 00:16:35,362
Then you're gonna see Pinocchio
tomorrow night.

141
00:16:36,488 --> 00:16:38,824
That's a wonderful way
to win over your children.

142
00:16:38,991 --> 00:16:42,995
I'm not serious. I'm just saying
that I grew up with Pinocchio.

143
00:16:43,161 --> 00:16:46,456
And the kids are still kids,
they're gonna eat it up.

144
00:16:47,666 --> 00:16:48,792
Okay.

145
00:16:49,001 --> 00:16:51,503
Okay, I'm wrong. I'm wrong.
I'm Wrong Roy, all right?

146
00:16:51,670 --> 00:16:55,632
Toby! You are close to death!
Come out here!

147
00:17:00,345 --> 00:17:05,058
Okay, now, I'll give you your choice.
I'm not gonna be biased in any way.

148
00:17:05,225 --> 00:17:09,521
You can either play Goofy Golf, which
means a lot of waiting and pushing...

149
00:17:09,688 --> 00:17:12,024
...or you can see Pinocchio...

150
00:17:12,190 --> 00:17:16,528
...which is a lot of furry animals and
magic and a wonderful time. Okay?

151
00:17:16,695 --> 00:17:18,613
- Now, let's vote.
- Golf!

152
00:17:18,822 --> 00:17:20,407
All right, everybody to bed!

153
00:17:20,574 --> 00:17:24,953
No way! Dad said we could finish
watching The Ten Commandments!

154
00:17:25,746 --> 00:17:28,248
Roy, that movie is four hours long.

155
00:17:28,415 --> 00:17:30,584
I told them
they'd watch only five commandments.

156
00:17:30,751 --> 00:17:32,544
- Hi, Ronnie, it's Earl.
- Hi, Earl.

157
00:17:32,711 --> 00:17:36,006
We got a big problem coming your way.
I need your old man in the field.

158
00:17:36,173 --> 00:17:38,216
Roy can't drive at night without me.

159
00:17:38,383 --> 00:17:40,635
Tell him we're experiencing
an overall outage.

160
00:17:40,802 --> 00:17:44,306
Wait, wait, wait, I can't...
Hey, honey.

161
00:17:44,473 --> 00:17:48,268
Jiminy Cricket!
It's Earl on the phone.

162
00:17:51,146 --> 00:17:52,689
Just a second.

163
00:17:52,856 --> 00:17:53,899
- Hello.
- Neary.

164
00:17:54,066 --> 00:17:57,360
Get into your truck
and report to line Mary-10 in Tolono.

165
00:17:57,527 --> 00:18:01,740
We're losing power across the grid.
Has the outage hit you yet?

166
00:18:01,907 --> 00:18:06,369
Neary? Neary? Neary?

167
00:18:14,211 --> 00:18:15,253
Hey! Lights!

168
00:18:17,422 --> 00:18:18,673
- Hey. Lights. Hey.
- Oh, God.

169
00:18:41,244 --> 00:18:43,997
Barry!

170
00:18:51,254 --> 00:18:53,548
Barry!

171
00:19:06,186 --> 00:19:08,313
Help! I'm lost.

172
00:19:08,521 --> 00:19:10,899
- Go, Ed.
- Responding to that 10-16...

173
00:19:11,066 --> 00:19:13,943
Hold on. You're gonna cough up a little
Tolono, right?

174
00:19:14,110 --> 00:19:17,280
Cough up a Tolono
or an interstate, a Highway 90.

175
00:19:17,447 --> 00:19:19,991
A little familiar landmark
of some sort.

176
00:19:20,450 --> 00:19:22,535
- Cornbread Road, south of 20.
- Cornbread?

177
00:19:22,702 --> 00:19:25,455
Sodium-vapor streetlights.
Power must have been restored.

178
00:19:25,622 --> 00:19:28,875
Couple hundred neighbors in their p.j.'s
think it's Saturday night.

179
00:19:29,042 --> 00:19:30,043
Dispatch, this is 411.

180
00:19:30,210 --> 00:19:33,338
Do you want me to disregard
that Tolono call?

181
00:19:36,424 --> 00:19:38,635
You're in the middle of the road,
jackass!

182
00:19:39,135 --> 00:19:42,514
Can you tell me where Cornbread is?
Turkey!

183
00:20:10,291 --> 00:20:13,711
A-34, A-34... F-27.

184
00:20:14,838 --> 00:20:18,174
M-Mary-10 through M-Mary-12.
Okay.

185
00:20:18,341 --> 00:20:20,218
Ten-47.

186
00:20:20,385 --> 00:20:23,138
Well, it's all meaningless so far.

187
00:20:23,304 --> 00:20:24,806
Okay. Come on.

188
00:20:25,014 --> 00:20:27,892
All right. Four-way stop leads you
to the single-circuits...

189
00:20:28,059 --> 00:20:30,520
...and leads you right to nowhere.

190
00:20:32,814 --> 00:20:34,440
M-Mary-10.

191
00:21:04,804 --> 00:21:09,475
Unit 211, 10-30, five, sev...

192
00:21:40,798 --> 00:21:42,508
Oh, shit.

193
00:23:26,992 --> 00:23:29,661
Oh, my God. Are you watching this?

194
00:23:29,828 --> 00:23:32,205
Don't look up at them,
they're like flashbulbs.

195
00:23:32,372 --> 00:23:37,002
Unit 211, I'm back up on the street.
What do you guys got out there?

196
00:23:37,461 --> 00:23:39,796
I don't believe this!

197
00:23:40,005 --> 00:23:42,466
My God! It's bigger than a house!

198
00:23:44,051 --> 00:23:46,345
This thing is like a barn.

199
00:23:46,928 --> 00:23:49,765
This is absolutely crazy.

200
00:23:51,975 --> 00:23:53,644
What is your location?

201
00:23:53,810 --> 00:23:57,564
Just off the Telemark Expressway
and east toward Harper Valley.

202
00:25:04,256 --> 00:25:06,091
Barry.

203
00:25:07,217 --> 00:25:08,468
No!

204
00:25:08,677 --> 00:25:09,928
Watch out!

205
00:25:15,225 --> 00:25:18,103
Oh, my God! Lady! Lady!

206
00:25:18,311 --> 00:25:20,147
Are you okay? I'm sorry.

207
00:25:20,313 --> 00:25:22,899
I didn't see him. He was just standing
in the middle of the road.

208
00:25:24,245 --> 00:25:26,081
- Hello?
- Oh, my God.

209
00:25:26,247 --> 00:25:30,418
- Hello! Here!
- What's the matter with you?

210
00:25:30,710 --> 00:25:34,547
Barry, come back here!
Come on, now.

211
00:26:04,828 --> 00:26:05,745
Ice cream!

212
00:26:26,492 --> 00:26:28,661
This is nuts.

213
00:26:29,536 --> 00:26:30,829
No.

214
00:27:11,511 --> 00:27:13,554
Wait a minute!

215
00:27:14,055 --> 00:27:16,474
What are you guys doing? Hey!

216
00:27:16,682 --> 00:27:19,435
That's Ohio! That's a quarter!

217
00:27:24,315 --> 00:27:28,236
Jesus, look at that. Look at those suckers.
They're glued to the road!

218
00:28:15,783 --> 00:28:17,827
Honey, wake up!

219
00:28:18,244 --> 00:28:21,414
Honey, Ronnie, wake up!
You're not gonna believe what I saw!

220
00:28:21,581 --> 00:28:23,332
- Ronnie?
- Don't! Not now.

221
00:28:23,499 --> 00:28:26,252
No, listen! Ronnie. Ronnie,
I never would have believed it.

222
00:28:26,419 --> 00:28:29,046
There was this...
In the cab, there was this whole...

223
00:28:29,213 --> 00:28:31,799
It went... It was...
There was a red whoosh that...

224
00:28:33,676 --> 00:28:35,052
Roy.

225
00:28:35,219 --> 00:28:36,637
Ike called you.

226
00:28:36,804 --> 00:28:38,890
- I gotta get something to wear.
- And McGovern.

227
00:28:39,056 --> 00:28:41,726
They want you to call right away.
They couldn't reach you.

228
00:28:42,143 --> 00:28:43,436
I turned the radio off.

229
00:28:43,603 --> 00:28:45,980
Don't do that.
The phone rang off the hook.

230
00:28:46,189 --> 00:28:49,817
It woke Sylvia up two times already.
And I think you'd better call them.

231
00:28:50,026 --> 00:28:53,196
You know those pictures in
National Geographic, the aurora borealis?

232
00:28:53,362 --> 00:28:56,115
This is better than that. This is really...
Come on!

233
00:28:56,324 --> 00:28:58,159
Come on, Ronnie, get up. Come on.

234
00:28:58,326 --> 00:29:00,536
- What's wrong?
- Nothing's wrong. I don't know.

235
00:29:00,703 --> 00:29:03,080
- Get up! I'm not kidding.
- Is it an accident?

236
00:29:03,247 --> 00:29:06,250
It's not an accident. You wanted
to get out of the house, right?

237
00:29:06,417 --> 00:29:08,127
Not at 4:00 in the morning.

238
00:29:08,294 --> 00:29:11,881
Ronnie, I need you
to see something with me.

239
00:29:12,048 --> 00:29:13,841
It's really important.

240
00:29:14,008 --> 00:29:16,594
Okay. All right.
What about the kids?

241
00:29:16,761 --> 00:29:18,679
What? Oh, the kids! I'll get the kids.

242
00:29:18,888 --> 00:29:20,932
Sylvia,
we're going on a little adventure.

243
00:29:21,307 --> 00:29:23,976
Toby! Brad! Come on! Get up.
Up. Come on.

244
00:29:24,185 --> 00:29:25,728
It's better than Goofy Golf!

245
00:29:29,065 --> 00:29:32,944
- Go faster.
- I'm going as fast as I can.

246
00:29:33,319 --> 00:29:34,904
Come on.

247
00:29:36,239 --> 00:29:39,659
All right, all right.
Oh, hi, Mrs. Harris.

248
00:29:39,825 --> 00:29:41,494
Roy, there's Mrs. Harris.

249
00:29:41,661 --> 00:29:45,414
There better be a good reason for this,
because it doesn't make any sense.

250
00:29:46,707 --> 00:29:50,670
All right, Roy, I really think
you've proved your point.

251
00:29:50,962 --> 00:29:53,714
Roy. Roy.

252
00:29:53,881 --> 00:29:56,634
Roy, you're sunburned. Look at you.

253
00:30:02,306 --> 00:30:04,475
Honey, what is that?

254
00:30:09,313 --> 00:30:11,607
Roy, what did it look like?

255
00:30:12,191 --> 00:30:14,610
It was like an ice-cream cone.

256
00:30:16,320 --> 00:30:17,780
What flavor?

257
00:30:17,947 --> 00:30:19,699
Orange.

258
00:30:20,157 --> 00:30:22,827
It was orange.
It wasn't like an ice-cream cone.

259
00:30:22,994 --> 00:30:25,288
It was more like a shell.
You know, like this:

260
00:30:25,454 --> 00:30:27,081
Like a taco?

261
00:30:27,248 --> 00:30:31,752
Was it like one of those Sara Lee
moon-shaped cookies?

262
00:30:31,919 --> 00:30:34,797
Those crescent cookies?

263
00:30:43,235 --> 00:30:46,112
Don't you think I'm taking this
really well?

264
00:30:49,366 --> 00:30:53,620
I remember when we used to come to places
like this just to look at each other.

265
00:30:57,958 --> 00:30:59,626
Snuggle.

266
00:33:25,188 --> 00:33:29,234
I don't believe it.
I don't believe it.

267
00:33:29,692 --> 00:33:31,277
I don't believe it.

268
00:33:31,444 --> 00:33:32,445
It's the Cotopaxi.

269
00:33:32,612 --> 00:33:35,865
- Why is it here?
- Beats the shit out of me.

270
00:34:10,446 --> 00:34:12,781
Is this the bathroom?

271
00:34:30,466 --> 00:34:33,761
Hey, this is not your toy to play with
every time I turn around.

272
00:34:33,928 --> 00:34:35,888
He looks like a 50/50 bar.

273
00:34:36,096 --> 00:34:37,597
All right, out of this house.
All of you. Out of my house.

274
00:34:37,598 --> 00:34:39,642
All right, out of this house.
All of you. Out of my house.

275
00:34:39,808 --> 00:34:42,144
I'm sorry, but you're
not to play with this, ever.

276
00:34:42,311 --> 00:34:45,147
Get out! While I clean up your mess.

277
00:34:45,314 --> 00:34:47,441
Thank you very much.

278
00:35:05,709 --> 00:35:08,254
Roy, I found
this overnight-tanning stuff.

279
00:35:08,420 --> 00:35:12,049
I want you to spray it on half
of your face, so it's all one color.

280
00:35:12,216 --> 00:35:15,636
It makes my face look yellow.
What does this remind you of, Ronnie?

281
00:35:16,220 --> 00:35:18,472
Then we'll tell everybody
you fell asleep...

282
00:35:18,639 --> 00:35:20,558
...under the sun lamp
on your right side.

283
00:35:20,724 --> 00:35:22,268
What for?

284
00:35:23,602 --> 00:35:26,272
- Dad, can I ask you a question?
- What?

285
00:35:26,438 --> 00:35:27,815
Are they for real?

286
00:35:27,982 --> 00:35:31,110
That's what for.
No, they are not for real.

287
00:35:31,277 --> 00:35:33,612
I want you to go outside
and have a hot dog, okay?

288
00:35:33,779 --> 00:35:35,864
Ronnie, don't tell him that.

289
00:35:36,031 --> 00:35:39,493
Roy, look, don't talk about this until
you know what you're talking about.

290
00:35:39,743 --> 00:35:43,622
Ronnie, that's crazy. If I can't talk about
it, how will I know what's going on?

291
00:35:43,789 --> 00:35:46,667
- What?
- Mom, I believe in them.

292
00:35:46,834 --> 00:35:50,337
- I believe in them too.
- Oh, no, you don't.

293
00:35:50,504 --> 00:35:52,214
Dad says we do.

294
00:35:52,381 --> 00:35:54,633
No, he does not.

295
00:35:54,967 --> 00:35:57,428
- Do you, Roy?
- Ronnie.

296
00:35:57,595 --> 00:36:01,015
All I wanna do is know
what's going on.

297
00:36:01,181 --> 00:36:04,435
What's...? Nothing's going on.
It's just one of those things.

298
00:36:04,602 --> 00:36:06,562
Which things? Which things?

299
00:36:07,229 --> 00:36:09,857
- I don't wanna hear about this.
- This is very important.

300
00:36:10,024 --> 00:36:13,068
I'm not just gonna let it lay here.
I'm gonna call somebody.

301
00:36:13,235 --> 00:36:15,404
- I'm not listening. I'm not listening.
- Ronnie.

302
00:36:15,613 --> 00:36:18,324
I saw something last night
that I can't explain.

303
00:36:20,117 --> 00:36:23,203
I saw something last night
I can't explain.

304
00:36:24,788 --> 00:36:27,458
I'm going out there
again tonight, you know.

305
00:36:29,251 --> 00:36:32,838
- No, you're not.
- Yes, I am.

306
00:36:33,005 --> 00:36:36,216
- No, you're not.
- Yes, I am.

307
00:36:38,427 --> 00:36:40,054
No, you're not.

308
00:36:40,220 --> 00:36:41,388
Do they live on the moon?

309
00:36:41,555 --> 00:36:45,726
They got bases on the moon so at night
they can come through your window.

310
00:36:45,893 --> 00:36:49,063
- This isn't a moonburn, goddamn it.
- You live on the moon.

311
00:36:49,229 --> 00:36:52,232
- I saw it. You came through my window.
- You hurt me!

312
00:36:52,399 --> 00:36:55,235
- You took all of my covers.
- Roy's not like that.

313
00:36:55,402 --> 00:36:57,905
He loves his work. Don't say that!

314
00:36:58,072 --> 00:37:00,658
What did he do?
He was with us all night!

315
00:37:00,824 --> 00:37:02,326
I won't have to go tonight.

316
00:37:02,493 --> 00:37:05,454
- He is not like that at all!
- I don't believe it.

317
00:37:05,621 --> 00:37:08,040
- Stop yelling at me!
- You did!

318
00:37:08,207 --> 00:37:10,334
- You did!
- Can't you tell him about this?

319
00:37:10,501 --> 00:37:11,043
Hello?

320
00:37:11,044 --> 00:37:12,127
Hello?

321
00:37:12,294 --> 00:37:13,837
I can't believe it.

322
00:37:14,004 --> 00:37:15,506
Roy...

323
00:37:15,673 --> 00:37:17,841
...you got fired.

324
00:37:18,425 --> 00:37:20,552
They didn't even wanna talk to you.

325
00:38:25,909 --> 00:38:29,913
I want to know... from what direction
did your people hear these sounds

326
00:38:35,085 --> 00:38:37,838
Where were the sounds
coming from?

327
00:39:03,614 --> 00:39:07,034
Where did these sounds come from?
Tell us, please.

328
00:39:16,126 --> 00:39:19,546
Where did these sounds come from?

329
00:39:34,061 --> 00:39:36,688
Good evening,
ladies and gentlemen.

330
00:39:39,775 --> 00:39:40,859
Well...

331
00:39:41,026 --> 00:39:45,280
I beg your pardon,
but my English is not good too.

332
00:39:47,783 --> 00:39:49,451
I want to share with you now...

333
00:39:49,618 --> 00:39:53,413
...the breakthrough
that happened in India.

334
00:39:53,789 --> 00:39:56,458
We think it means something.

335
00:39:57,543 --> 00:40:00,671
We think it is important.

336
00:40:00,963 --> 00:40:06,510
To help you learn, I am using the hand
sign created by Zoltán Kodály.

337
00:40:07,219 --> 00:40:10,472
Kodály developed these signs
to teach music to deaf children.

338
00:40:51,638 --> 00:40:53,765
And now...

339
00:41:26,465 --> 00:41:28,050
I haven't had a hand all night.

340
00:41:37,351 --> 00:41:39,186
Hello?

341
00:41:39,728 --> 00:41:40,771
Hi.

342
00:41:40,979 --> 00:41:43,315
Oh, hi, how are you?
You came here too?

343
00:41:43,482 --> 00:41:45,108
Yes. Jillian Guiler.

344
00:41:45,275 --> 00:41:47,569
Roy Neary.
I'm sorry about what happened.

345
00:41:47,819 --> 00:41:51,239
- Good night!
- What?

346
00:41:52,407 --> 00:41:54,785
Look at you.

347
00:41:57,454 --> 00:41:59,957
You got the same burn.

348
00:42:00,123 --> 00:42:04,086
Yeah, but it's better on you.
You got it all over at least.

349
00:42:04,252 --> 00:42:06,880
I gotta tan the other side tonight.

350
00:42:07,255 --> 00:42:09,508
How's the kid? I'm really sorry.

351
00:42:09,675 --> 00:42:11,093
I'll be right back.

352
00:42:11,301 --> 00:42:14,304
Raise up your head a bit
and give me a smile. Come on, son.

353
00:42:14,471 --> 00:42:18,100
He's a little young for a record,
don't you think?

354
00:42:37,577 --> 00:42:39,204
Weird.

355
00:42:40,664 --> 00:42:45,669
I know this sounds crazy,
but ever since yesterday on the road...

356
00:42:45,961 --> 00:42:48,338
...I've been seeing this shape.

357
00:42:50,257 --> 00:42:53,301
In shaving cream and pillows...

358
00:43:05,647 --> 00:43:09,526
Damn it, I know this!
I know what this is.

359
00:43:15,240 --> 00:43:17,534
This means something.

360
00:43:23,540 --> 00:43:25,751
This is important.

361
00:43:26,251 --> 00:43:29,087
- Here they come!
- Get the camera!

362
00:43:29,254 --> 00:43:32,591
- Out of the northwest!
- Jimmy, come here.

363
00:43:46,646 --> 00:43:49,816
It's like Halloween for grownups.

364
00:43:51,151 --> 00:43:52,611
Trick or treat.

365
00:44:23,642 --> 00:44:24,935
Wait a minute.

366
00:45:18,113 --> 00:45:19,156
Here are the patterns.

367
00:45:19,408 --> 00:45:23,037
- We received two 15-minute broadcasts.
- Non-random signals?

368
00:45:23,204 --> 00:45:26,916
A hundred and four rapid pulses.
After a five-second interval, 40 pulses.

369
00:45:27,083 --> 00:45:29,460
Another five-second break,
and 30 pulses.

370
00:45:31,212 --> 00:45:33,589
Sixty seconds of silence,
then a new set of numbers.

371
00:45:33,756 --> 00:45:36,467
Forty, break five, 36, break five, 10.

372
00:45:36,676 --> 00:45:38,928
- A hundred and four rapid pulses.
- Lord!

373
00:45:39,095 --> 00:45:42,890
Wait 60 seconds and the
whole doggone thing repeats.

374
00:45:43,349 --> 00:45:46,352
- Where are these signals coming from?
- Right in the neighborhood.

375
00:45:46,519 --> 00:45:48,354
Light-travel time, roughly 7 seconds.

376
00:45:48,521 --> 00:45:50,815
It's well within the plane
of the ecliptic.

377
00:45:50,982 --> 00:45:55,987
Are these non-random signals here,
40, 36, 10, in response to that?

378
00:45:56,153 --> 00:45:59,407
No. They should be. We've sent out
this musical combination for weeks.

379
00:45:59,573 --> 00:46:01,659
But all we're getting back
are numbers.

380
00:46:07,123 --> 00:46:08,873
This could mean the India sounds
reached a dead end.

381
00:46:08,874 --> 00:46:10,960
This could mean the India sounds
reached a dead end.

382
00:46:11,127 --> 00:46:12,545
They don't mean a thing.

383
00:46:12,753 --> 00:46:14,797
There's still so much
that we don't know.

384
00:46:15,006 --> 00:46:17,717
How about
where the signals originate?

385
00:46:20,636 --> 00:46:23,222
- Excuse me.
- Maybe they're letters.

386
00:46:24,223 --> 00:46:26,809
What letter is 104?

387
00:46:28,728 --> 00:46:30,062
Excuse me.

388
00:46:33,190 --> 00:46:36,986
Before I got paid to speak French...

389
00:46:37,653 --> 00:46:39,196
...I...

390
00:46:39,363 --> 00:46:41,657
I used to read maps.

391
00:46:43,117 --> 00:46:45,953
This first number is a longitude.

392
00:46:49,790 --> 00:46:52,585
Yeah. Two sets of three numbers.

393
00:46:52,752 --> 00:46:55,796
Degrees, minutes and seconds.

394
00:46:56,005 --> 00:47:00,217
Now, the first number has three digits,
and the last two are below 60.

395
00:47:01,093 --> 00:47:04,388
Obviously, it's not in the right ascension
and declination on the sky.

396
00:47:05,848 --> 00:47:08,267
These have to be Earth coordinates.

397
00:47:08,434 --> 00:47:10,353
There's a globe in the office.

398
00:47:13,814 --> 00:47:15,149
This is what we need.

399
00:47:15,316 --> 00:47:18,027
Maybe there's a release pin
underneath here.

400
00:47:18,194 --> 00:47:20,571
That's a $2500 globe.
What are you guys doing?

401
00:47:20,738 --> 00:47:23,574
- It's heavy, watch your feet.
- Let me hit the mother.

402
00:47:38,672 --> 00:47:42,843
All right, everybody, quiet down!
Hold it down! Quiet!

403
00:47:45,888 --> 00:47:47,056
That's not right.

404
00:47:47,223 --> 00:47:49,767
The antenna. How dumb!

405
00:47:49,975 --> 00:47:51,644
- Which way's it pointing?
- Northeast.

406
00:47:51,811 --> 00:47:53,646
That's Wyoming.

407
00:48:00,194 --> 00:48:02,696
We're going to need a geodetic
survey map of Wyoming.

408
00:48:02,863 --> 00:48:04,824
I want this down to the square yard.

409
00:48:05,032 --> 00:48:08,160
Get me a geodetic
survey map of Wyoming.

410
00:48:08,327 --> 00:48:10,413
We not only need...

411
00:49:59,897 --> 00:50:02,024
Toys!

412
00:50:02,650 --> 00:50:04,944
Toys!

413
00:50:36,517 --> 00:50:38,269
Come on, come on.

414
00:51:21,812 --> 00:51:23,355
Oh, my God.

415
00:51:51,967 --> 00:51:54,386
You can come and play now.

416
00:51:54,553 --> 00:51:56,513
You can come and play now.

417
00:52:01,769 --> 00:52:04,688
Come in through the door.

418
00:52:06,398 --> 00:52:09,610
One, two, three...

419
00:52:18,494 --> 00:52:19,828
Oh, honey.

420
00:52:20,037 --> 00:52:22,164
Mom, look! The sun's here!

421
00:52:25,250 --> 00:52:27,378
Oh, honey.

422
00:52:28,045 --> 00:52:30,172
Oh, my God!

423
00:53:07,835 --> 00:53:09,503
Go away!

424
00:53:33,652 --> 00:53:35,154
Clean everything up!

425
00:53:51,336 --> 00:53:53,338
- Mom, let me listen.
- Wait a minute! Wait.

426
00:54:11,190 --> 00:54:12,733
- Mom, look!
- Oh, my God.

427
00:54:21,283 --> 00:54:24,161
Come in through the door.

428
00:54:45,641 --> 00:54:49,686
Barry! Barry!

429
00:54:59,909 --> 00:55:01,285
Jillian. Jillian.

430
00:55:04,247 --> 00:55:05,581
May we have a statement?

431
00:55:05,748 --> 00:55:08,626
The report you gave to the police
was really quite spectacular.

432
00:55:08,793 --> 00:55:11,003
We need it for the 6:00 news.

433
00:55:11,212 --> 00:55:13,256
- Jillian.
- Mrs. Guiler.

434
00:55:13,589 --> 00:55:14,966
They got him.

435
00:55:15,132 --> 00:55:16,175
What?

436
00:55:16,384 --> 00:55:18,886
Is it true you're leaving
to look for your son?

437
00:55:19,053 --> 00:55:22,682
- Has there been a ransom note?
- Where would you start looking?

438
00:55:24,976 --> 00:55:26,936
His name is Barry.

439
00:55:27,687 --> 00:55:29,897
Where is your husband now,
Mrs. Guiler?

440
00:55:30,106 --> 00:55:31,148
Mrs. Guiler.

441
00:55:31,315 --> 00:55:35,152
Are you involved in any
kind of parapsychologies?

442
00:55:35,945 --> 00:55:38,364
Turn around, please. Turn around!

443
00:55:40,074 --> 00:55:44,036
You gave the police a report. Would you
mind repeating it for the television?

444
00:55:47,331 --> 00:55:50,585
Folks, you can go in now.
Room 3655.

445
00:55:50,877 --> 00:55:54,255
Ladies and gentlemen,
this is a flying saucer.

446
00:55:54,422 --> 00:55:55,715
That's the one I saw.

447
00:55:58,509 --> 00:56:02,221
I swear that's the same one.
It's the one I saw.

448
00:56:03,014 --> 00:56:05,766
It's made of pewter,
made in Japan...

449
00:56:05,933 --> 00:56:09,270
...and thrown across the lawn
by one of my children.

450
00:56:10,813 --> 00:56:15,151
I wanted to point out that we're
not all "polished brass" about this.

451
00:56:15,359 --> 00:56:18,279
And also to make a point
that last year...

452
00:56:18,487 --> 00:56:21,449
...Americans shot more than
7 billion photographs...

453
00:56:21,616 --> 00:56:26,662
...at a record of $6.6 billion
for film equipment and processing.

454
00:56:26,829 --> 00:56:28,831
Now, with all those shutters clicking...

455
00:56:28,998 --> 00:56:31,792
...where is the indisputable
photographic evidence?

456
00:56:32,001 --> 00:56:35,254
Major Benchley, I've been in the news
business for a long time.

457
00:56:35,421 --> 00:56:39,550
Our cameras have never taken a picture
of a plane crash as it actually happened...

458
00:56:39,717 --> 00:56:43,638
...or an automobile accident
and get it on the 6:00 news.

459
00:56:44,013 --> 00:56:46,223
- I have seen lights.
- Of course you have.

460
00:56:47,183 --> 00:56:48,935
Folks...

461
00:56:51,354 --> 00:56:54,899
Now, there are all kinds of ideas
that would be fun to believe in.

462
00:56:55,066 --> 00:56:56,817
Mental telepathy...

463
00:56:56,984 --> 00:56:58,819
...time travel...

464
00:56:58,986 --> 00:57:00,696
...immortality...

465
00:57:01,238 --> 00:57:03,282
...even Santa Claus.

466
00:57:03,616 --> 00:57:07,912
I know it's no fun to go home and say,
"Guess what happened today.

467
00:57:08,079 --> 00:57:11,832
I was in the shopping center,
there was this tremendously bright light...

468
00:57:11,999 --> 00:57:14,585
...and I rushed outside...

469
00:57:15,211 --> 00:57:16,587
...and it was an airplane."

470
00:57:16,796 --> 00:57:19,799
Excuse me, sir.
I didn't want to see this.

471
00:57:20,967 --> 00:57:22,385
I sure wish I had.

472
00:57:22,551 --> 00:57:24,553
For 15 years, I've been looking...

473
00:57:24,720 --> 00:57:27,974
...for these damn silly lights
in the night sky.

474
00:57:28,140 --> 00:57:29,684
I've never found any.

475
00:57:29,850 --> 00:57:32,853
I'd like to, because
I believe in life elsewhere.

476
00:57:33,145 --> 00:57:34,605
Why don't you guys admit...

477
00:57:34,814 --> 00:57:38,442
...that the Air Force is conducting
secret tests in the Foothills area?

478
00:57:38,609 --> 00:57:41,612
It would be easy to say yes to that.

479
00:57:42,029 --> 00:57:45,950
But I'm not going to mislead you.
This is not the case.

480
00:57:46,325 --> 00:57:48,411
I don't know what you saw.

481
00:57:48,619 --> 00:57:50,913
You can't fool us
by agreeing with us.

482
00:57:51,080 --> 00:57:52,498
No, no, no. Come on!

483
00:57:52,915 --> 00:57:54,959
I saw Bigfoot once.

484
00:57:59,588 --> 00:58:03,968
1951.
Back in Sequoia National Park.

485
00:58:04,176 --> 00:58:08,431
Had a foot on her, 37 inches.
Heel to toe.

486
00:58:12,143 --> 00:58:15,938
Made a sound I would not want
to hear twice in my life.

487
00:58:20,609 --> 00:58:23,612
I believe I've encountered
that gentleman myself.

488
00:58:24,739 --> 00:58:27,241
I was sitting on the porch,
looking through the screen.

489
00:58:27,408 --> 00:58:29,952
I seen this big white dot in the sky.

490
00:58:30,161 --> 00:58:32,455
It turned red and blue and green.

491
00:58:34,081 --> 00:58:36,876
...It just kept pulsating
more and more and more.

492
00:58:37,043 --> 00:58:42,381
UFOs do not represent a direct
physical threat to our national security.

493
00:58:43,132 --> 00:58:47,178
We do not support them,
and we encourage you not to.

494
00:59:25,591 --> 00:59:27,718
Let's get in touch with
the Forest Service...

495
00:59:27,885 --> 00:59:31,806
...or we'll end up in a wilderness
area with vehicular traffic.

496
00:59:31,972 --> 00:59:36,185
And that's strictly sacred-cow stuff
to those folks in Wyoming.

497
00:59:42,233 --> 00:59:45,194
If this mission fully develops...

498
00:59:45,778 --> 00:59:50,991
...I get white-knuckled just thinking about
what might be ahead for those folks.

499
00:59:51,700 --> 00:59:53,661
Your flight will be at 2000 hours.

500
00:59:53,828 --> 00:59:57,873
We have a limo that'll take you
to the heliport now.

501
00:59:58,040 --> 01:00:01,877
And, of course, transportation
will be provided on the other end.

502
01:00:03,212 --> 01:00:06,799
- Look. I still like the flash-flood idea.
- Where do you get the water?

503
01:00:06,966 --> 01:00:08,592
We've had about two inches
of rain...

504
01:00:08,759 --> 01:00:12,429
We'll tell them one of the reservoirs
is gonna burst.

505
01:00:12,596 --> 01:00:15,141
Besides, there's not enough water
in those reservoirs.

506
01:00:15,307 --> 01:00:18,811
Wait. Contaminated water
affects people, crops, animals.

507
01:00:19,019 --> 01:00:20,104
Disease.

508
01:00:21,438 --> 01:00:23,065
- Yeah, epidemic.
- What kind of disease?

509
01:00:23,232 --> 01:00:25,526
- A plague epidemic.
- Nobody will believe a plague.

510
01:00:27,236 --> 01:00:28,863
- Anthrax.
- Ranching country.

511
01:00:29,029 --> 01:00:31,407
- Yes.
- There are a lot of sheep up in those hills.

512
01:00:31,574 --> 01:00:33,284
Wait a minute. Good, I like that.

513
01:00:33,492 --> 01:00:36,495
- It'll create a panic.
- But it may not evacuate everybody.

514
01:00:36,662 --> 01:00:39,957
There's always some joker
who thinks he's immune.

515
01:00:41,709 --> 01:00:45,921
What I need is something so scary
it'll clear 300 square miles...

516
01:00:46,130 --> 01:00:49,049
...of every living Christian soul.

517
01:00:58,350 --> 01:00:59,351
Come on.

518
01:01:20,247 --> 01:01:22,041
Help yourself.

519
01:01:24,001 --> 01:01:29,465
I hate these potatoes.
There's a dead fly in my potatoes.

520
01:01:29,632 --> 01:01:31,300
It's okay.

521
01:03:17,364 --> 01:03:19,700
Well, I guess you've noticed...

522
01:03:20,200 --> 01:03:25,039
...something is a little strange
with Dad.

523
01:03:27,875 --> 01:03:29,793
It's okay, though.

524
01:03:31,962 --> 01:03:33,964
I'm still Dad.

525
01:03:38,594 --> 01:03:42,765
I can't describe it,
what I'm feeling...

526
01:03:42,973 --> 01:03:45,184
...and what I'm thinking.

527
01:03:47,895 --> 01:03:50,481
This means something.

528
01:03:51,398 --> 01:03:53,776
This is important.

529
01:04:04,745 --> 01:04:06,872
That's not right.

530
01:04:10,376 --> 01:04:12,211
It's not right.

531
01:04:13,962 --> 01:04:15,964
That's not right.

532
01:04:34,650 --> 01:04:38,195
That's not right!

533
01:04:44,118 --> 01:04:46,245
What is it?

534
01:04:47,454 --> 01:04:49,957
What is it?

535
01:04:57,214 --> 01:04:59,258
It's not fair.

536
01:05:04,388 --> 01:05:06,306
Tell me.

537
01:05:30,831 --> 01:05:32,416
Roy?

538
01:05:33,208 --> 01:05:34,751
Roy.

539
01:05:35,711 --> 01:05:37,880
Open the door, please.

540
01:05:38,630 --> 01:05:42,759
Please. Roy. Roy!

541
01:05:42,926 --> 01:05:44,720
Open this door!

542
01:05:44,928 --> 01:05:47,181
Open this door!

543
01:05:52,478 --> 01:05:54,062
All right.

544
01:06:15,167 --> 01:06:17,503
I don't think I know
what's happening to me.

545
01:06:24,092 --> 01:06:28,931
Look, Roy. What it is,
is family therapy.

546
01:06:29,097 --> 01:06:31,808
I mean, we all go. We all talk.

547
01:06:32,017 --> 01:06:35,979
No one is singled out.
Maybe it's not your fault anyway.

548
01:06:36,146 --> 01:06:40,901
- It's waterproof. It still works.
- Roy! Promise me you'll go!

549
01:06:41,109 --> 01:06:44,530
- Please!
- You crybaby! You crybaby!

550
01:06:44,738 --> 01:06:46,615
- You crybaby!
- Get out of here!

551
01:06:46,823 --> 01:06:50,661
- Get out! Get out!
- Come on, you guys. Come on.

552
01:06:50,827 --> 01:06:52,579
You crybaby!

553
01:06:52,788 --> 01:06:54,373
- Be quiet!
- Stop it!

554
01:06:54,581 --> 01:06:56,250
I don't understand what this is!

555
01:06:56,416 --> 01:06:58,835
- Well, neither do I.
- Get out!

556
01:06:59,002 --> 01:07:02,256
Go to your room and close the door.

557
01:07:02,422 --> 01:07:04,007
Hon?

558
01:07:05,008 --> 01:07:06,093
- Ronnie?
- Yes.

559
01:07:06,260 --> 01:07:08,303
I'm really scared.

560
01:07:08,470 --> 01:07:10,597
I want you to help me.
Come on. Ronnie?

561
01:07:10,764 --> 01:07:13,892
All this bullshit!
It's turning this house upside down!

562
01:07:14,059 --> 01:07:16,395
- Ronnie, just...
- I just hate you! That's why!

563
01:07:16,562 --> 01:07:18,230
- No!
- Ronnie. Ronnie.

564
01:07:18,397 --> 01:07:21,942
Just hold me. Just put your arms
around me. It'll really help me.

565
01:07:22,150 --> 01:07:23,151
It'll really help me.

566
01:07:23,318 --> 01:07:26,613
Listen! Listen! Listen!
Don't you see what's happening?

567
01:07:26,780 --> 01:07:28,865
- None of our friends call us anymore.
- Ronnie.

568
01:07:29,032 --> 01:07:31,118
You're out of work. You don't care.

569
01:07:31,285 --> 01:07:34,204
You're wrecking us!
You're ruining us!

570
01:07:34,371 --> 01:07:36,957
- Ronnie, just hold on.
- No! No!

571
01:07:37,124 --> 01:07:39,376
- Hold on.
- No!

572
01:07:40,544 --> 01:07:42,379
Ronnie.

573
01:07:43,005 --> 01:07:44,715
Ronnie?

574
01:07:46,008 --> 01:07:49,386
Ronnie, open the door now.
Come on.

575
01:08:24,379 --> 01:08:27,674
Well, what do you know?
It's disintegrated.

576
01:08:30,594 --> 01:08:34,765
Happy birthday, you thing from
another world, you.

577
01:08:35,057 --> 01:08:37,100
Thank you!

578
01:08:43,690 --> 01:08:45,817
Well, I guess he's had enough.

579
01:08:48,028 --> 01:08:50,614
Are you going to yell at me?

580
01:09:06,171 --> 01:09:09,091
Ronnie? Everything's fine.

581
01:09:19,267 --> 01:09:21,728
All this stuff is coming down.

582
01:09:26,316 --> 01:09:29,611
It's all gonna be like it was.

583
01:09:37,119 --> 01:09:42,666
That's the last straw!
Now I use my secret weapon!

584
01:10:11,611 --> 01:10:13,405
Roy?

585
01:10:13,572 --> 01:10:17,826
Roy, I'm sorry about last night. Roy!

586
01:10:18,744 --> 01:10:22,831
Roy. Roy, what are you doing?

587
01:10:23,540 --> 01:10:25,333
Jesus!

588
01:10:30,172 --> 01:10:33,133
Roy. Roy.

589
01:10:34,426 --> 01:10:35,719
The plants are fine there!

590
01:10:37,012 --> 01:10:38,305
Roy, can you hear me?

591
01:10:38,930 --> 01:10:40,140
Wait!

592
01:10:40,307 --> 01:10:42,350
Wait! What are you doing?

593
01:10:42,559 --> 01:10:43,769
You're gonna love it!

594
01:10:43,935 --> 01:10:46,396
- What do you mean?
- You'll really love this.

595
01:10:46,563 --> 01:10:49,649
Roy, what are you doing?

596
01:10:54,654 --> 01:10:57,115
Oh, Roy!

597
01:10:57,282 --> 01:10:58,325
My God!

598
01:10:59,910 --> 01:11:01,745
Come on, men!

599
01:11:02,871 --> 01:11:07,501
Dad, after this, can we
throw dirt in my window?

600
01:11:10,670 --> 01:11:12,339
Stop it.

601
01:11:13,298 --> 01:11:15,091
Stop. Stop it.

602
01:11:15,258 --> 01:11:16,676
Ronnie...

603
01:11:17,302 --> 01:11:21,932
...if I don't do this, that's when
I'm gonna need a doctor.

604
01:11:24,935 --> 01:11:26,353
Do what? What are you doing?

605
01:11:26,561 --> 01:11:28,396
- I got it.
- What are you doing?

606
01:11:28,563 --> 01:11:31,900
I figured it out, that's all.
Will you just listen?

607
01:11:32,067 --> 01:11:37,072
You ever look at something crazy, and
then see it another way and it's not?

608
01:11:37,239 --> 01:11:38,281
No, I haven't.

609
01:11:38,448 --> 01:11:40,408
Come on, Ronnie. Just listen.

610
01:11:40,575 --> 01:11:42,869
- I don't know what you're doing.
- Close your eyes.

611
01:11:43,036 --> 01:11:46,331
Close your eyes and hold your breath,
everything will be really pretty.

612
01:11:46,498 --> 01:11:47,749
Roy?

613
01:11:47,916 --> 01:11:48,959
You're scaring us now.

614
01:11:49,125 --> 01:11:52,295
Don't be scared. Just don't be scared.
I feel really good.

615
01:11:52,504 --> 01:11:54,214
Everything's gonna be all right.

616
01:11:54,381 --> 01:11:56,633
I haven't felt this good in years.

617
01:11:56,800 --> 01:11:59,052
Everything's gonna be okay.
I can feel it.

618
01:12:02,430 --> 01:12:05,892
- Now what else? What else?
- Like this? Like this?

619
01:12:06,476 --> 01:12:07,978
Right.

620
01:12:46,683 --> 01:12:48,435
Like this?

621
01:12:50,854 --> 01:12:52,355
Chicken wire.

622
01:13:02,782 --> 01:13:04,784
Whatever you're doing,
it's against the law!

623
01:13:04,951 --> 01:13:06,703
He's putting it back, Mrs. Harris.

624
01:13:06,870 --> 01:13:07,913
I'll pay you for this.

625
01:13:08,079 --> 01:13:09,122
Take it. Take it.

626
01:13:09,289 --> 01:13:11,249
I'm perfectly fine.

627
01:13:11,458 --> 01:13:13,460
You let go of this fence.
You come with me.

628
01:13:13,627 --> 01:13:16,546
Do you want me to hit you?
You let go of that fence!

629
01:13:16,713 --> 01:13:21,384
Toby! Listen to me. Listen to me. Please.
Come on, right now. We're going.

630
01:13:21,551 --> 01:13:24,554
- I want to stay here!
- Let go of the fence! Come on.

631
01:13:42,781 --> 01:13:44,950
Stay! Stay!

632
01:13:48,912 --> 01:13:51,414
- Where are we going?
- Where are we going?

633
01:13:51,581 --> 01:13:53,249
- Where are we going?
- Get in the car.

634
01:13:53,416 --> 01:13:56,419
- Where are we going?
- Quietly, quickly, get in.

635
01:13:56,586 --> 01:13:58,213
Get in there, Toby.

636
01:13:58,380 --> 01:14:00,006
Quickly.

637
01:14:02,467 --> 01:14:05,011
- Dad needs some more help.
- Okay, lock the doors.

638
01:14:05,178 --> 01:14:06,596
Dad needs some more help!

639
01:14:06,763 --> 01:14:10,266
Quiet! Just lock the doors.
Roll up those windows.

640
01:14:10,433 --> 01:14:13,103
Dad needs some more help!

641
01:14:13,311 --> 01:14:14,813
Honey, where you going?

642
01:14:14,980 --> 01:14:17,232
- What?
- Where are you going?

643
01:14:17,691 --> 01:14:20,276
Taking the kids to my sister's.

644
01:14:21,528 --> 01:14:23,321
That's crazy. You're not even dressed.

645
01:14:24,906 --> 01:14:26,199
What?

646
01:14:26,366 --> 01:14:28,201
You said what?

647
01:14:32,872 --> 01:14:34,582
Ronnie, wait!

648
01:14:34,791 --> 01:14:36,918
Ronnie, hold it. Hold it.

649
01:14:37,085 --> 01:14:39,754
Can you hold...
Would you hold it one minute?

650
01:15:06,990 --> 01:15:08,033
Morning.

651
01:15:47,363 --> 01:15:48,489
What is wrong with me today?

652
01:15:48,490 --> 01:15:50,867
What is wrong with me today?

653
01:15:56,706 --> 01:15:57,957
Who is it, please?

654
01:15:58,124 --> 01:16:02,712
It's the mailman. There's a package
for Julie Anderson.

655
01:16:05,632 --> 01:16:08,968
You can just leave it at the door.

656
01:16:10,386 --> 01:16:14,682
I can't do that.
I'm supposed to c.o.d. Like that.

657
01:16:14,849 --> 01:16:16,643
And besides...

658
01:16:17,227 --> 01:16:19,896
...you and I have to have
a little talk...

659
01:16:20,647 --> 01:16:22,524
...about Kim.

660
01:16:41,835 --> 01:16:44,754
Like sands through the hourglass...

661
01:16:44,921 --> 01:16:48,174
...so are the Days of Our Lives.

662
01:17:13,723 --> 01:17:17,685
Good evening. At the top of the news
tonight, a rail disaster.

663
01:17:17,852 --> 01:17:19,770
- Hang up.
- At Devils Tower, Wyoming...

664
01:17:19,937 --> 01:17:23,357
...a train loaded with a dangerous
chemical gas went off the rails...

665
01:17:23,524 --> 01:17:26,193
...and has forced the widest
area evacuation...

666
01:17:26,360 --> 01:17:29,655
...in the history of these
controversial Army rail shipments.

667
01:17:29,822 --> 01:17:33,284
Whenever you want me to.
Tomorrow is fine.

668
01:17:33,451 --> 01:17:34,785
...To the public for three weeks...

669
01:17:34,952 --> 01:17:37,622
- I can do other things.
- ...for renovations in the national park.

670
01:17:37,872 --> 01:17:41,417
- I'm trying to be.
- We go to Charles McDonald for a report.

671
01:17:41,584 --> 01:17:42,710
Yes, I am.

672
01:17:42,877 --> 01:17:45,963
The Army and National Guard
are supervising the evacuation.

673
01:17:46,130 --> 01:17:50,635
I am an adult, okay?
Even though there's no such thing.

674
01:17:50,801 --> 01:17:52,553
It was a joke!

675
01:17:52,720 --> 01:17:55,806
...The danger will be over
within 72 hours.

676
01:17:55,973 --> 01:17:58,976
Ronnie, anything you want.

677
01:17:59,143 --> 01:18:02,021
Anything you want, I'll do.

678
01:18:04,732 --> 01:18:06,609
For how long?

679
01:18:11,405 --> 01:18:15,076
- Once the toxic concentration's down to...
- All of you?

680
01:18:15,242 --> 01:18:20,623
No. Ronnie, I wanna talk
about this in person.

681
01:18:20,790 --> 01:18:25,544
This is not... We can't talk
about this on the telephone. All right...

682
01:18:25,711 --> 01:18:28,631
Ronnie! Don't hang up!
Just don't...

683
01:18:28,798 --> 01:18:34,762
...meat should remain unaffected. This
means order your steak well-done.

684
01:18:34,929 --> 01:18:37,890
Devils Tower, Wyoming was
the first national monument...

685
01:18:38,057 --> 01:18:42,228
...erected in this country by
Theodore Roosevelt in 1915.

686
01:18:42,436 --> 01:18:45,982
Thousands of civilian refugees
are fleeing the area...

687
01:18:46,148 --> 01:18:49,610
...spurred on by rumors that the
seven overturned tanker cars...

688
01:18:49,819 --> 01:18:54,365
...at Walkashi Needles Junction were
filled to capacity with GM nerve gas.

689
01:18:54,532 --> 01:18:56,200
In a few minutes,
we're going to be...

690
01:18:56,367 --> 01:18:59,704
...boasts 400,000 vacationers.

691
01:18:59,870 --> 01:19:02,832
Fortunately, during this mishap
there have been no fatalities.

692
01:19:02,999 --> 01:19:05,584
...Forced to evacuate this area,
which soon...

693
01:19:05,793 --> 01:19:09,296
...will become the "hot zone,"
depending on the prevailing winds.

694
01:19:09,463 --> 01:19:13,509
But as it is, this is as close to the
disaster as we've been allowed to get.

695
01:19:13,718 --> 01:19:16,470
...Due to a strong prevailing
north-south wind.

696
01:19:16,637 --> 01:19:19,473
Army chemical engineers and the
Wyoming National Guard...

697
01:19:19,640 --> 01:19:23,144
...are making every effort to both
contain the leaking toxins...

698
01:19:23,352 --> 01:19:26,605
...and evacuate an area of almost
200 square miles.

699
01:19:26,772 --> 01:19:30,151
Everyone is being warned:
Stay out of the area.

700
01:19:30,317 --> 01:19:33,195
Everyone, please,
stay out of the area.

701
01:19:33,404 --> 01:19:35,698
...And thousands of others
are homeless.

702
01:19:35,865 --> 01:19:40,202
U.S. Army Materiel Command has
issued these new area restrictions:

703
01:19:40,369 --> 01:19:42,621
All roadways north of Crowheart
on Interstate 25.

704
01:19:42,788 --> 01:19:43,914
Shit.

705
01:19:44,081 --> 01:19:47,543
All roads leading into the
Grand Tetons west of Meeteeste.

706
01:19:47,710 --> 01:19:50,671
All multilane undivided
full-traffic interchange...

707
01:19:50,838 --> 01:19:54,759
...railroad, local and historic
stage roads south of Cody...

708
01:20:05,019 --> 01:20:08,105
Why aren't there any fat lines
instead of these thin ones?

709
01:20:09,440 --> 01:20:10,608
Yo!

710
01:20:12,443 --> 01:20:14,361
- Holy mother of...
- You're going the wrong way!

711
01:20:15,154 --> 01:20:19,992
Yo! Turn back around.
Back this way.

712
01:20:20,159 --> 01:20:21,869
Idiot! What's the matter with you?

713
01:20:22,078 --> 01:20:23,704
Yes, sir. Sorry, officer.

714
01:20:23,913 --> 01:20:26,749
It's just a rental, officer.
Just a rental.

715
01:20:27,583 --> 01:20:28,918
My fault! My fault!

716
01:20:29,173 --> 01:20:31,467
Starting with the letter "F,"
as in "Frank"...

717
01:20:31,717 --> 01:20:33,886
...to the letter "J" as in "Jack"...

718
01:20:34,095 --> 01:20:38,933
...please find your way to the loading
platform for immediate boarding.

719
01:20:42,103 --> 01:20:44,480
You'll be real disappointed
and sorry...

720
01:20:44,647 --> 01:20:49,151
...if you don't have an early-warning
system, such as a bird, a gas mask.

721
01:20:49,318 --> 01:20:51,612
Why, even my dog has a gas mask.

722
01:20:51,779 --> 01:20:53,530
And any of you folks are
worth more than a dog.

723
01:20:53,531 --> 01:20:54,407
And any of you folks are
worth more than a dog.

724
01:20:57,076 --> 01:21:01,205
Roy? Roy!

725
01:21:02,331 --> 01:21:04,333
Roy!

726
01:21:05,876 --> 01:21:09,463
The train has time to clear the area.

727
01:21:09,630 --> 01:21:14,343
There's no immediate danger
and no need to hurry or push.

728
01:21:14,510 --> 01:21:17,388
Please help each other
by not rushing.

729
01:21:17,555 --> 01:21:18,806
Roy?

730
01:21:21,934 --> 01:21:23,311
Roy?

731
01:21:26,063 --> 01:21:27,648
Roy!

732
01:21:28,899 --> 01:21:30,484
Roy.

733
01:21:33,321 --> 01:21:36,449
- Roy!
- Come on up here.

734
01:21:39,410 --> 01:21:43,372
I don't care where you go, just get behind
the barricades! I don't want any suicides.

735
01:21:43,581 --> 01:21:45,791
There's no way out!

736
01:22:09,106 --> 01:22:10,107
What are we doing?

737
01:22:10,274 --> 01:22:13,736
Only way to get back is cross-country.
We'll break through the fence.

738
01:22:13,903 --> 01:22:15,988
- Let's go.
- All right, hold on. Hold on.

739
01:22:31,234 --> 01:22:32,527
Watch out!

740
01:22:40,034 --> 01:22:42,453
The police dragged the river
for him.

741
01:22:42,620 --> 01:22:45,623
I told them he wasn't in the river.
He wasn't in the river.

742
01:22:45,832 --> 01:22:50,837
Then they went around to every house
within five miles...

743
01:22:51,504 --> 01:22:56,009
...looking inside
backyard refrigerators.

744
01:22:59,512 --> 01:23:03,224
They asked me if I'd seen any strangers
in the neighborhood.

745
01:24:11,084 --> 01:24:13,711
I don't believe it's real.

746
01:24:15,505 --> 01:24:17,965
I don't believe it's real.

747
01:24:18,800 --> 01:24:20,551
It's real.

748
01:24:26,808 --> 01:24:30,144
Let's get down there. Get some gas
and get down in there.

749
01:24:33,231 --> 01:24:34,399
What are those?

750
01:24:58,631 --> 01:25:03,177
Look, I guarantee you.
This whole thing is a put-on.

751
01:25:38,671 --> 01:25:39,922
Oh, damn it!

752
01:25:45,845 --> 01:25:47,722
Ma'am, would you please step away...

753
01:25:47,889 --> 01:25:49,640
- This is for your own safety.
- Okay.

754
01:25:49,807 --> 01:25:52,226
All right. What do you want?
Get your hands off me!

755
01:25:54,395 --> 01:25:57,273
- How are you feeling?
- I feel fine!

756
01:25:57,523 --> 01:26:00,985
According to my birds, the only bad air
here is you guys farting around!

757
01:26:11,954 --> 01:26:13,790
- Move them out!
- What's happening?

758
01:26:13,956 --> 01:26:16,459
- What do you want? Where are we going?
- Go to the van.

759
01:26:16,626 --> 01:26:17,960
Where are you taking her?

760
01:26:18,127 --> 01:26:20,087
- Hold it! Why can't we go together?
- Roy?

761
01:26:20,505 --> 01:26:21,589
Watch your step.

762
01:26:22,089 --> 01:26:23,591
Roy!

763
01:26:39,690 --> 01:26:42,944
We have very little time, Mr. Neary.
This is Mr. Lacombe.

764
01:26:43,110 --> 01:26:45,321
- Hello.
- We need answers from you...

765
01:26:45,488 --> 01:26:47,657
...that are honest, direct
and to the point.

766
01:26:47,824 --> 01:26:48,950
Where is Jillian?

767
01:26:51,118 --> 01:26:54,330
Do you realize the danger you
and your friend have risked?

768
01:26:55,331 --> 01:26:58,543
In coming here, you've
exposed yourself to toxic gas.

769
01:26:58,709 --> 01:27:00,586
Well, I'm alive.

770
01:27:00,753 --> 01:27:02,630
We're talking.

771
01:27:03,089 --> 01:27:05,299
This is true, Mr. Neary.

772
01:27:05,925 --> 01:27:09,428
But if the wind blew from
the south instead of the north...

773
01:27:10,847 --> 01:27:13,015
...this conversation would not be
taking place.

774
01:27:13,224 --> 01:27:15,351
There's nothing wrong with the air.

775
01:27:16,477 --> 01:27:18,229
What makes you say that?

776
01:27:18,396 --> 01:27:20,982
I just know.
There's nothing wrong with it.

777
01:27:21,148 --> 01:27:23,734
Go outside and make me a liar.

778
01:27:27,530 --> 01:27:30,157
Look, I wanna talk
to the man in charge.

779
01:27:30,324 --> 01:27:32,785
Mr. Lacombe
is the highest authority.

780
01:27:32,952 --> 01:27:34,912
He isn't even an American.

781
01:27:37,164 --> 01:27:40,543
Mr. Neary, are you an artist
or a painter?

782
01:27:41,377 --> 01:27:42,461
No.

783
01:27:45,965 --> 01:27:49,135
Have you been hearing a persistent
ringing in your ears?

784
01:27:49,302 --> 01:27:51,137
Almost an agreeable ringing?

785
01:27:51,304 --> 01:27:52,805
No.

786
01:27:53,848 --> 01:27:56,350
Having headaches, migraines?

787
01:27:56,517 --> 01:27:57,977
Yeah.

788
01:27:58,853 --> 01:28:00,855
An irritation in your eyes
and your sinuses?

789
01:28:01,022 --> 01:28:02,273
Yeah.

790
01:28:03,441 --> 01:28:06,944
Do you have hives?
Do you have allergies?

791
01:28:08,654 --> 01:28:10,781
And burning on your face
and on your body?

792
01:28:10,948 --> 01:28:13,784
- Yes. Who are you people?
- Look at this.

793
01:28:13,951 --> 01:28:17,038
Yeah, I got one just like it in my
living room. Who are you people?

794
01:28:22,793 --> 01:28:25,713
Monsieur Neary, please,
one more question.

795
01:28:27,048 --> 01:28:29,717
Have you recently had
a close encounter?

796
01:28:31,010 --> 01:28:34,221
A close encounter
with something very unusual?

797
01:28:36,766 --> 01:28:38,893
Who are you people?

798
01:28:45,483 --> 01:28:49,946
- These people strangers to you?
- Yeah. Except for her.

799
01:28:51,614 --> 01:28:54,951
And the two of you felt compelled
to be here?

800
01:28:55,660 --> 01:28:57,745
Yeah, you might say that.

801
01:28:58,037 --> 01:29:01,624
- But what did you expect to find?
- An answer.

802
01:29:02,959 --> 01:29:05,169
That's not crazy, is it?

803
01:29:21,060 --> 01:29:24,146
Hold it, hold it, hold it.

804
01:29:24,313 --> 01:29:25,940
Is that it?

805
01:29:26,524 --> 01:29:29,110
Is that all you're gonna ask me?

806
01:29:29,819 --> 01:29:32,405
Well, I got a couple of thousand
goddamn questions.

807
01:29:32,571 --> 01:29:36,951
I wanna speak to someone in charge.
I wanna lodge a complaint.

808
01:29:37,118 --> 01:29:39,453
You have no right
to make people crazy.

809
01:29:39,620 --> 01:29:43,791
You think I investigate every
Walter Cronkite story there is?

810
01:29:44,333 --> 01:29:47,878
If this is just nerve gas, how come I
know everything in such detail?

811
01:29:48,045 --> 01:29:50,214
I've never been here before.

812
01:29:50,798 --> 01:29:52,842
How come I know so much?

813
01:29:53,009 --> 01:29:55,011
What the hell is going on
around here?

814
01:29:55,177 --> 01:29:57,596
Who the hell are you people?

815
01:30:22,163 --> 01:30:24,540
Is that it? Answer me!

816
01:30:24,999 --> 01:30:28,711
I didn't come this far to get
taken on any bus ride home!

817
01:30:50,983 --> 01:30:53,402
Five minutes. Five!

818
01:30:53,694 --> 01:30:56,113
We're gonna hold
for a couple of minutes.

819
01:31:29,897 --> 01:31:33,400
You brought in 12 people to
the decontamination camp...

820
01:31:33,567 --> 01:31:38,072
...instead of the evacuation center
where they belong. I'd like to know why.

821
01:31:38,239 --> 01:31:41,492
- Because this means something.
- Because this means something.

822
01:31:42,868 --> 01:31:46,455
These people have come from
all over their country...

823
01:31:47,832 --> 01:31:51,335
...to a place they have been told
will endanger their lives.

824
01:31:51,502 --> 01:31:52,586
- Why?
- Why?

825
01:31:52,795 --> 01:31:56,507
Well, because somebody could be trying
to subvert this whole operation...

826
01:31:56,757 --> 01:32:00,261
...by sending in fanatics, cultists
and Christ knows what all.

827
01:32:01,137 --> 01:32:02,680
Yes.

828
01:32:03,597 --> 01:32:05,641
Thank you very much.

829
01:32:07,893 --> 01:32:10,813
This is a small group of people...

830
01:32:11,897 --> 01:32:15,568
...who have shared a vision
in common.

831
01:32:15,734 --> 01:32:17,153
Look.

832
01:32:20,030 --> 01:32:23,033
It's still a mystery to me
why they are here.

833
01:32:23,242 --> 01:32:25,286
Even they do not know why.

834
01:32:43,971 --> 01:32:45,598
Jillian!

835
01:32:46,724 --> 01:32:48,893
- Jillian.
- Don't! You'll be poisoned!

836
01:32:49,059 --> 01:32:52,605
Listen, there's nothing wrong
with the air around here.

837
01:32:52,771 --> 01:32:56,609
The Army's getting us out of here
because they don't want any witnesses.

838
01:32:57,276 --> 01:33:00,613
But if the Army doesn't want us here,
it's none of our business.

839
01:33:00,821 --> 01:33:04,408
We only wanted to see the mountain.
It was a coincidence when I painted it.

840
01:33:04,617 --> 01:33:06,327
You can take it off.

841
01:33:06,493 --> 01:33:10,039
The air here is better
than it is in Los Angeles.

842
01:33:10,206 --> 01:33:14,001
Listen. How many of you people are
for getting out of here?

843
01:33:19,548 --> 01:33:23,510
I don't know what's happening either,
and I must find out what's going on.

844
01:33:27,848 --> 01:33:32,853
I believe for every one of these anxious,
anguished people who've come here...

845
01:33:35,064 --> 01:33:40,152
...there must be hundreds also touched
by the vision who never made it here.

846
01:33:40,819 --> 01:33:43,906
Simply because they never watch
the television.

847
01:33:45,324 --> 01:33:48,661
Or perhaps they watched it, but never
made the psychic connection.

848
01:33:48,869 --> 01:33:52,706
It's a coincidence. It's not scientific.

849
01:33:55,209 --> 01:33:57,086
Listen to me, Major Walsh...

850
01:33:57,253 --> 01:34:00,130
...it is an event sociological.

851
01:34:18,691 --> 01:34:20,901
Hey! Wait a minute!

852
01:34:36,964 --> 01:34:38,799
- My name's Neary!
- Larry Butler!

853
01:34:38,966 --> 01:34:40,926
- How you doing?
- I'm doing okay! You?

854
01:34:41,093 --> 01:34:43,011
- Fine, fine!
- That's good!

855
01:34:43,512 --> 01:34:48,016
Everybody keep their masks on until
you're out of here and out of danger.

856
01:34:48,183 --> 01:34:52,354
Listen, we wanna get out of here!
What's the matter with you?

857
01:35:07,786 --> 01:35:11,039
- Major Walsh, Major Walsh.
- Yes, sir.

858
01:35:11,206 --> 01:35:13,792
I'll have the rest of them
off the mountain in one hour.

859
01:35:13,959 --> 01:35:16,712
Major, I want you to use infrared...

860
01:35:16,879 --> 01:35:19,506
...and get me a photo analysis of
the northern face.

861
01:35:19,673 --> 01:35:21,800
Yes, sir, it's already been ordered.

862
01:35:21,967 --> 01:35:24,052
Major Walsh, listen.

863
01:35:24,219 --> 01:35:27,890
I don't like to do this. We're in
enough trouble with these cattlemen.

864
01:35:28,056 --> 01:35:31,768
But if you can't get these folks
out of the area by 2000 hours...

865
01:35:31,935 --> 01:35:34,980
...start dusting with EZ-4.

866
01:35:35,147 --> 01:35:36,398
What's EZ-4?

867
01:35:36,565 --> 01:35:39,943
It's a sleep aerosol. Same stuff
that we used with the livestock.

868
01:35:40,110 --> 01:35:41,612
Comes out of Riot Control.

869
01:35:41,778 --> 01:35:45,657
They'll sleep for six hours and
wake up with a headache, that's all.

870
01:35:45,824 --> 01:35:49,703
We didn't choose this place.
We didn't choose these people!

871
01:35:49,870 --> 01:35:52,623
They were invited!

872
01:35:58,128 --> 01:36:00,380
They belong here more than we.

873
01:36:08,221 --> 01:36:09,848
I shouldn't have given up jogging.

874
01:36:10,015 --> 01:36:13,226
See that notch in the mountain?
We can make that one in no time.

875
01:36:13,393 --> 01:36:15,562
Here come three more.

876
01:36:18,190 --> 01:36:20,150
The authority in this park...

877
01:36:20,317 --> 01:36:23,987
...has been superseded by
the United States government.

878
01:36:24,154 --> 01:36:26,865
There's a ravine that leads straight up.

879
01:36:27,032 --> 01:36:30,035
- It's an easier climb. It faces northeast.
- It's no good.

880
01:36:30,202 --> 01:36:33,830
When you get to the top, it's
a 300-foot drop straight down. Get down!

881
01:36:33,997 --> 01:36:37,000
What do you think is
down on the other side?

882
01:36:37,250 --> 01:36:40,295
There's a box canyon on
the other side with ravines and trails...

883
01:36:40,462 --> 01:36:42,923
...if you take this gradual incline
to the right.

884
01:36:43,090 --> 01:36:45,759
I never imagined that.
I only painted one side.

885
01:36:45,926 --> 01:36:49,054
- There was no canyon in my doodles.
- Next time, sculpt.

886
01:36:49,221 --> 01:36:52,140
You give us a lot of credit.
That's a good hour's climb.

887
01:36:52,307 --> 01:36:53,517
Come on, let's go.

888
01:36:59,022 --> 01:37:01,608
The authority in this park...

889
01:37:01,775 --> 01:37:06,029
...has been superseded by
the United States government.

890
01:37:06,196 --> 01:37:10,909
You are entering
a military reservation...

891
01:37:11,118 --> 01:37:13,870
...and subject to military law.

892
01:37:14,037 --> 01:37:16,081
Call the bastard.

893
01:37:19,459 --> 01:37:21,586
Bahama, this is Pyramid. Over.

894
01:37:21,753 --> 01:37:22,921
Go ahead.

895
01:37:23,088 --> 01:37:25,465
Nothing to report at midstation.

896
01:37:25,632 --> 01:37:28,593
As it looks up ahead,
there's thousands of places to hide.

897
01:37:28,760 --> 01:37:32,889
We're gonna need at least three times
the men to cover this in one hour.

898
01:37:33,056 --> 01:37:36,059
Get everybody off the northern face,
call the dark side of the moon...

899
01:37:36,226 --> 01:37:38,145
...and tell them we're gonna dust.

900
01:38:04,921 --> 01:38:06,631
Get down!

901
01:38:07,299 --> 01:38:10,093
You are entering a military
reservation...

902
01:38:10,302 --> 01:38:14,306
...and subject to military law.

903
01:38:43,418 --> 01:38:46,755
Larry! Larry, come on!

904
01:39:03,939 --> 01:39:07,109
Larry! Don't let them see you!
Get down!

905
01:39:42,894 --> 01:39:46,898
They're just crop-dusting.
Los Angeles!

906
01:39:50,944 --> 01:39:54,072
- Larry?
- Don't stop!

907
01:39:54,364 --> 01:39:55,656
Don't look back! Come on, come on!

908
01:39:55,657 --> 01:39:56,199
Don't look back! Come on, come on!

909
01:40:20,015 --> 01:40:21,391
Roy!

910
01:40:27,689 --> 01:40:30,525
Hurry! We're only 10 feet
from the top.

911
01:40:30,734 --> 01:40:33,778
Just a few more feet!
We can slide down the other side!

912
01:40:38,491 --> 01:40:39,784
No!

913
01:40:41,077 --> 01:40:43,580
Come on, hurry. I'll come down.

914
01:40:43,747 --> 01:40:44,831
Don't come down.

915
01:40:45,749 --> 01:40:47,626
Come on, come on. Come on!

916
01:40:47,792 --> 01:40:49,920
- Don't worry. We're gonna make it.
- Roy.

917
01:40:50,086 --> 01:40:52,214
Come on. Come on. Move it.
Move your ass!

918
01:40:52,422 --> 01:40:53,840
Come on.

919
01:40:58,511 --> 01:41:02,224
Roy, come on. Come on.
Please, come on.

920
01:41:03,350 --> 01:41:05,018
Hurry!

921
01:41:06,728 --> 01:41:07,854
Hold it!

922
01:41:11,983 --> 01:41:13,151
Jump! Jump!

923
01:41:26,164 --> 01:41:29,459
- Do you see that?
- Oh, yes.

924
01:41:29,918 --> 01:41:31,086
Good.

925
01:41:32,045 --> 01:41:34,714
Is it on?
Check the speaker system here.

926
01:41:34,881 --> 01:41:38,760
- I want this up all the way.
- Is this one live?

927
01:41:40,011 --> 01:41:42,055
Are you reading me on this one?

928
01:41:42,347 --> 01:41:43,390
How about here?

929
01:41:45,850 --> 01:41:50,397
- Testing. Testing.
- Okay, one, two, three, four.

930
01:41:51,064 --> 01:41:52,816
Testing. Testing.

931
01:41:52,983 --> 01:41:56,695
One, two, three, testing.

932
01:42:03,493 --> 01:42:07,789
Gentlemen, ladies.
Take your positions, please.

933
01:42:08,498 --> 01:42:10,583
This is not a drill.

934
01:42:10,750 --> 01:42:14,504
I repeat, this is not a drill.

935
01:42:15,171 --> 01:42:19,175
Could we have the lights in the arena
down 60 percent, please?

936
01:42:19,342 --> 01:42:21,261
Sixty percent.

937
01:42:25,223 --> 01:42:28,226
I don't think we could ask for
a more beautiful evening, do you?

938
01:42:37,902 --> 01:42:40,697
Okay, watch the skies, please.

939
01:42:40,947 --> 01:42:46,286
We now show uncorrelated targets
approaching from the north-northwest.

940
01:43:35,835 --> 01:43:39,005
We're the only ones who know.
The only ones.

941
01:43:48,598 --> 01:43:50,266
You see that?

942
01:43:52,102 --> 01:43:53,812
Yes.

943
01:43:54,104 --> 01:43:55,230
Good.

944
01:44:43,611 --> 01:44:48,241
All ground personnel not
D-class approved...

945
01:44:48,408 --> 01:44:52,537
...please evacuate
the center approach marker.

946
01:44:52,704 --> 01:44:57,417
Audio analysis personnel,
behind the double yellow line.

947
01:45:03,256 --> 01:45:06,176
ITC, stereo, time and resistance.

948
01:45:06,384 --> 01:45:08,178
Auto ready?

949
01:45:08,511 --> 01:45:11,097
- Tone interpolation on interlock.
- Altitude and tabling.

950
01:45:11,264 --> 01:45:13,892
ARP interlock now.

951
01:45:14,058 --> 01:45:16,603
Speed set at seven and a half.

952
01:45:16,769 --> 01:45:20,315
All positive functions, standing by.

953
01:45:26,571 --> 01:45:30,825
Special ARP teams
tagged C, "Captain," F, "Frank"...

954
01:45:30,992 --> 01:45:33,411
...to the speaking platform.

955
01:45:43,379 --> 01:45:45,798
- Six-two.
- Go ahead.

956
01:45:45,965 --> 01:45:47,175
- Sunset.
- Go.

957
01:45:48,301 --> 01:45:50,970
Okay. Start with the tone.

958
01:45:57,977 --> 01:45:59,187
- Tang.
- Go.

959
01:46:00,146 --> 01:46:02,190
Up a full tone.

960
01:46:05,151 --> 01:46:06,986
Down a major third.

961
01:46:07,403 --> 01:46:09,405
Now drop an octave.

962
01:46:14,327 --> 01:46:15,620
- Cool blue.
- Go.

963
01:46:15,828 --> 01:46:17,872
Up a perfect fifth.

964
01:46:20,375 --> 01:46:22,335
Nothing. Nothing at all.

965
01:46:22,544 --> 01:46:27,006
We have a possible anomaly ranging
from plus-5 milligals to minus-50...

966
01:46:27,173 --> 01:46:28,675
Give me a tone.

967
01:46:28,841 --> 01:46:31,094
- "Re" to the second.
- Up a full tone.

968
01:46:31,261 --> 01:46:33,304
- "Mi" to the third.
- Down a major third.

969
01:46:33,471 --> 01:46:35,557
- "Do" to the first.
- Drop an octave.

970
01:46:35,723 --> 01:46:37,433
- "Do" one-half.
- Up a perfect fifth.

971
01:46:37,600 --> 01:46:39,561
"So" to the fifth.

972
01:46:55,451 --> 01:46:56,744
I know that.

973
01:46:59,455 --> 01:47:01,040
Faster.

974
01:47:16,472 --> 01:47:17,640
Kick that mule!

975
01:47:21,811 --> 01:47:23,479
Let's go!

976
01:48:05,021 --> 01:48:06,773
I'm very happy tonight.

977
01:48:06,939 --> 01:48:08,816
- Congratulations.
- Thank you.

978
01:48:08,983 --> 01:48:12,695
Not a Merle Haggard,
but it was great.

979
01:48:32,173 --> 01:48:34,050
Mr. Lacombe?

980
01:48:40,139 --> 01:48:43,309
What is it? What's happening?

981
01:49:38,656 --> 01:49:41,743
Come on. Come on, let's go.

982
01:49:42,744 --> 01:49:44,287
Keep down.

983
01:49:50,001 --> 01:49:51,669
They're talking way over my head.

984
01:51:01,239 --> 01:51:03,199
- Shit.
- Duck, duck!

985
01:51:39,235 --> 01:51:40,611
Photo one.

986
01:51:40,778 --> 01:51:44,115
Fantastic!
Keep those reloads coming.

987
01:51:44,282 --> 01:51:45,950
Fantastic!

988
01:51:52,540 --> 01:51:54,709
- You wanna see better?
- I can see fine.

989
01:51:54,917 --> 01:51:58,045
- We can't stay here.
- I can.

990
01:51:58,880 --> 01:52:02,592
- Why?
- Because Barry's not here.

991
01:52:03,384 --> 01:52:05,469
I'm just not ready.

992
01:52:09,223 --> 01:52:11,475
I can't stay.
I've gotta get down there.

993
01:52:11,684 --> 01:52:13,477
I know.

994
01:52:46,636 --> 01:52:51,098
This is Data Control
to all personnel.

995
01:52:51,265 --> 01:52:54,644
We monitor no biologic hazards.

996
01:52:54,810 --> 01:52:57,104
Range safety clear.

997
01:54:04,672 --> 01:54:07,550
Oh, my God.

998
01:55:00,853 --> 01:55:03,272
Oh, my God.

999
01:55:26,962 --> 01:55:28,380
Holy shit.

1000
01:57:20,409 --> 01:57:22,703
There is a safety-hazard zone...

1001
01:57:22,870 --> 01:57:26,957
...extending 25 meters
from the ship.

1002
01:57:27,166 --> 01:57:32,046
Special teams are exempt and
should be aware of low gravity.

1003
01:57:32,254 --> 01:57:36,550
Expect some dizziness,
and look out for a static charge.

1004
01:57:38,969 --> 01:57:42,848
All departments at operational
during this phase...

1005
01:57:43,057 --> 01:57:45,267
...signify by beeping twice.

1006
01:57:45,434 --> 01:57:48,729
If everything's ready here on
the dark side of the moon...

1007
01:57:48,896 --> 01:57:50,522
...play the five tones.

1008
01:58:59,925 --> 01:59:02,970
Give her six quavers, then pause.

1009
01:59:03,637 --> 01:59:05,139
She sent us four quavers...

1010
01:59:05,306 --> 01:59:08,642
...a group of five quavers,
a group of four semiquavers.

1011
01:59:23,032 --> 01:59:25,326
All these phrases have
in common are five signals.

1012
01:59:25,492 --> 01:59:28,829
- I hope somebody's taking this down.
- What are we saying to each other?

1013
01:59:28,996 --> 01:59:31,832
It seems they're trying to teach us
a basic tonal vocabulary.

1014
01:59:31,999 --> 01:59:33,375
It's the first day of school.

1015
01:59:41,675 --> 01:59:44,720
Take everything from the lady.
Follow her pattern note for note.

1016
02:00:01,487 --> 02:00:03,989
We have a translation interlock
on their audio signal.

1017
02:00:04,156 --> 02:00:06,450
We're taking over
this conversation now.

1018
02:01:08,429 --> 02:01:09,054
Let's go! Come on! Come on!

1019
02:01:09,055 --> 02:01:10,264
Let's go! Come on! Come on!

1020
02:02:46,902 --> 02:02:49,112
I am Claude Lacombe.

1021
02:02:52,199 --> 02:02:53,617
Frank Taylor...

1022
02:02:53,784 --> 02:02:56,370
...Lieutenant J.G., United States
Navy Reserve...

1023
02:02:56,537 --> 02:02:58,497
...064199.

1024
02:02:58,664 --> 02:03:01,333
Lieutenant, welcome home.

1025
02:03:01,500 --> 02:03:03,460
This way to debriefing.

1026
02:03:03,627 --> 02:03:04,711
Harry Ward Craig...

1027
02:03:04,920 --> 02:03:07,089
...Captain, United States Navy...

1028
02:03:07,256 --> 02:03:09,550
...043431.

1029
02:03:09,716 --> 02:03:13,095
Captain, would you come
right this way?

1030
02:03:14,805 --> 02:03:17,724
Welcome back, Navy.
Welcome back.

1031
02:03:19,268 --> 02:03:22,437
- Craig, Harry Ward.
- "Captain, United States Navy...

1032
02:03:22,604 --> 02:03:24,731
...0434311 ."

1033
02:03:24,898 --> 02:03:28,527
Disappeared off Chicken Shoals,
flight 19.

1034
02:03:30,070 --> 02:03:33,824
Matthew McMichael, Lieutenant,
United States Naval Reserve...

1035
02:03:34,283 --> 02:03:36,785
- ...0909411.
- Lieutenant.

1036
02:03:36,952 --> 02:03:38,787
Good to have you back.

1037
02:03:39,454 --> 02:03:41,623
Furlow, Otis B...

1038
02:03:41,790 --> 02:03:44,918
...Lieutenant J.G.,
United States Navy.

1039
02:03:45,085 --> 02:03:47,963
They haven't even aged.
Einstein was right.

1040
02:03:48,130 --> 02:03:50,591
Einstein was probably one of them.

1041
02:03:51,049 --> 02:03:53,176
Erickson, David A...

1042
02:03:53,343 --> 02:03:56,722
...Lieutenant J.G.,
United States Navy...

1043
02:03:56,888 --> 02:04:01,268
...0639552.

1044
02:04:01,935 --> 02:04:04,229
Henkle, Robert F...

1045
02:04:04,396 --> 02:04:07,899
...Lieutenant J.G.,
United States Navy...

1046
02:04:08,066 --> 02:04:12,070
...0618475.

1047
02:04:35,302 --> 02:04:40,390
...Reported missing July 1955,
Pensacola, Florida.

1048
02:04:40,557 --> 02:04:42,351
Monsieur Neary...

1049
02:04:42,726 --> 02:04:44,394
...what do you want?

1050
02:04:47,230 --> 02:04:50,442
I just wanna know that
it's really happening.

1051
02:04:53,654 --> 02:04:58,659
I went up in the air,
and I saw our house.

1052
02:04:58,825 --> 02:05:01,828
I saw you going up in the air.
Did you see me running after you?

1053
02:05:01,995 --> 02:05:04,081
- Yes.
- Yes?

1054
02:05:08,251 --> 02:05:10,712
All right, now we have to talk.

1055
02:05:13,090 --> 02:05:15,175
He wants to speak about
Mr. Neary's case.

1056
02:05:15,384 --> 02:05:18,095
Swensen, Matthew Daniel...

1057
02:05:18,261 --> 02:05:21,848
...reported missing April 1976.

1058
02:05:22,557 --> 02:05:25,894
- Are they going away?
- Yes.

1059
02:05:29,606 --> 02:05:30,941
Wait a second, wait.

1060
02:05:31,108 --> 02:05:35,237
He says these are ordinary people
under extraordinary circumstances.

1061
02:05:36,363 --> 02:05:39,574
Delores, Rudolph E...

1062
02:05:39,741 --> 02:05:43,412
...reported missing
December '51, Bisbee...

1063
02:05:43,912 --> 02:05:45,831
...Arizona.

1064
02:05:49,376 --> 02:05:51,420
Goodbye.

1065
02:06:01,722 --> 02:06:02,764
Monsieur Neary...

1066
02:06:04,766 --> 02:06:06,685
...I envy you.

1067
02:08:04,719 --> 02:08:07,556
Mr. Neary, I'm told we can count on your
complete cooperation.

1068
02:08:07,806 --> 02:08:10,225
- What's your blood type?
- I don't know.

1069
02:08:10,392 --> 02:08:13,144
- What's your birth date?
- December 4th, 1944.

1070
02:08:13,311 --> 02:08:15,814
Have you been inoculated against
smallpox, diphtheria?

1071
02:08:15,981 --> 02:08:19,276
Is there any history of liver disease
in your family?

1072
02:09:18,209 --> 02:09:20,795
May the Lord be praised
at all times.

1073
02:09:20,962 --> 02:09:23,715
May God help us and grant us
a happy journey.

1074
02:09:23,924 --> 02:09:25,258
Show us your ways.

1075
02:09:25,425 --> 02:09:27,344
And lead us along your paths.

1076
02:09:27,552 --> 02:09:31,139
God has given you
his angels charge over you.

1077
02:09:31,348 --> 02:09:34,935
Grant these pilgrims, we pray,
a happy journey...

1078
02:09:35,143 --> 02:09:40,065
...and peaceful days, so that with
your holy angel as a guide...

1079
02:09:40,231 --> 02:09:44,069
...they may safely reach their
destination...

1080
02:13:25,056 --> 02:13:26,808
Bye!

